4. STATISTICAL ANALYSIS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  TWO-WAY ANOVA (Algorithm × Environment)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Note: The algorithm main effect (C(algorithm)) produced NaN
  for all metrics due to RRT*'s 0% success rate in maze making
  the design matrix rank-deficient. Results for environment
  main effect and interaction are valid.

  Metric                Env Effect (F)   Interaction (F)   Interaction p
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  path_length           181.16           0.012             0.988  (n.s.)
  time_ms               1031.88          1852.09           < 0.001 ★★★
  nodes_explored        479.87           38.69             < 0.001 ★★★
  smoothness            204.73           0.048             0.953  (n.s.)
  altitude_utilisation  350.98           1.479             0.228  (n.s.)

  Interpretation:
  • Environment type significantly affects every metric.
  • The algorithm × environment INTERACTION is only significant
    for time_ms and nodes_explored — meaning how much slower
    RRT* is depends heavily on which environment it's in.
  • Path length and smoothness show no significant interaction:
    the algorithm's advantage is consistent across environments.


  COHEN'S D EFFECT SIZES (A* vs RRT*, successful trials only)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Metric        Random    Canyon    Clustered    Interpretation
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  path_length   +0.926    +0.261    +0.493       RRT* shorter (medium–large)
  time_ms       −4.414    −6.864    −4.546       A* faster (very large)

  Cohen's d convention: small ≥ 0.2, medium ≥ 0.5, large ≥ 0.8
  Positive d = A* higher; negative d = RRT* higher.
  The time_ms effect sizes (d ≈ −4.4 to −6.9) are exceptional —
  the distributions barely overlap.


